## Why This Is Asked
This question explores runtime extensibility and undoable actions through a practical pattern common in tooling and scripting domains.

## Key Concepts
- **Command pattern** with undo support
- **Runtime plugin registry** for lazy loading
- **Safe macro/sequence composition** with transactional semantics
- **Isolation and error handling** for plugins

## Code Example
```typescript
// types.ts
export interface Context {
  args: string[]
  env: Record<string, string>
}

export interface Command {
  name: string
  execute(ctx: Context): Promise<void>
  undo(ctx: Context): Promise<void>
}

export type CommandFactory = (name: string) => Command | Promise<Command>

// registry.ts
export class Registry {
  private factories = new Map<string, CommandFactory>()
  private commands = new Map<string, Command>()

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory)
  }

  async get(name: string): Promise<Command> {
    if (!this.commands.has(name)) {
      const factory = this.factories.get(name)
      if (!factory) throw new Error(`Command "${name}" not found`)
      this.commands.set(name, await factory(name))
    }
    return this.commands.get(name)!
  }
}

// macro.ts
export class Macro implements Command {
  name: string
  private commands: Command[]

  constructor(name: string, commands: Command[]) {
    this.name = name
    this.commands = commands
  }

  async execute(ctx: Context): Promise<void> {
    const executed: Command[] = []
    try {
      for (const cmd of this.commands) {
        await cmd.execute(ctx)
        executed.push(cmd)
      }
    } catch (err) {
      for (const cmd of executed.reverse()) {
        await cmd.undo(ctx)
      }
      throw err
    }
  }

  async undo(ctx: Context): Promise<void> {
    for (const cmd of this.commands.reverse()) {
      await cmd.undo(ctx)
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design a plug-in style, runtime-extensible CLI using classic design patterns with a minimal surface area.

## Key Concepts

- **Registry Pattern**: Maps command names to factories for dynamic lookup
- **Factory Pattern**: Creates command instances on demand
- **Dispatcher**: Resolves and executes commands with provided arguments
- **Conflict Handling**: Strategy-based approach (overwrite, warn, or reject)
- **Help Generation**: Aggregates help() output from all registered commands

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Command {
  execute(args: string[]): Promise<any>;
  help(): string;
}

type CommandFactory = () => Command;

class CommandRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, CommandFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory, allowOverwrite = false): void {
    if (this.factories.has(name) && !allowOverwrite) {
      throw new Error(`Command '${name}' already registered`);
    }
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  getFactory(name: string): CommandFactory | undefined {
    return this.factories.get(name);
  }

  getAllCommands(): string[] {
    return Array.from(this.factories.keys());
  }
}

class Dispatcher {
  constructor(private registry: CommandRegistry) {}

  async dispatch(name: string, args: string[]): Promise<any> {
    const factory = this.registry.getFactory(name);
    if (!factory) {
      throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${name}`);
    }
    return factory().execute(args);
  }

  help(): string {
    const commands = this.registry.getAllCommands();
    return commands.map(name => {
      const factory = this.registry.getFactory(name)!;
      return factory().help();
    }).join('\n');
  }
}

